# Schema: audit-access.sh
#
# Documents the audit script so an agent unfamiliar with it can run and
# interpret it.

## Purpose

Enumerates Docker API endpoint groups against a running docker-socket-proxy
and prints an allowed/denied matrix. Acceptance tests A-2 (and the periodic
policy re-check) run this script.

## Interface

- Invocation: `audit-access.sh <proxy-base-url>` (e.g. `http://socket-proxy:2375`)
- Must execute from a container attached to the proxy's internal network
  (R-5 means the endpoint exists nowhere else).
- Exit 0: matrix printed. Exit 1: endpoint unreachable (proxy down or
  caller not on the network).

## Interpretation

| Output | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `ALLOWED` (2xx) | Group enabled; must match the documented allowlist (R-2) |
| `denied` (403) | Group disabled; the expected state for everything unlisted |
| `unexpected(NNN)` | 5xx: daemon or proxy misbehaving; 000: timeout - investigate, do not assume policy |

## Rules the script follows

- POSIX sh, curl only: runs in any consumer container with a shell.
- Probes are representative requests per group, never destructive: the
  exec/build probes target nonexistent resources and expect 403 on a
  correctly-scoped proxy.
- Never prints response bodies (they can carry container names/labels);
  only the verdict.
